WEBVTT

00:00.690 --> 00:02.450
- [Instructor] We're
told, this is the graph

00:02.450 --> 00:04.420
of y is equal to 3/2 to the x.

00:04.420 --> 00:06.060
And that's it right over there.

00:06.060 --> 00:09.150
Use the graph to find
an approximate solution

00:09.150 --> 00:12.170
to 3/2 to the x is equal to five.

00:12.170 --> 00:14.280
So pause this video and
try to do this on your own

00:14.280 --> 00:16.530
before we work on this together.

00:16.530 --> 00:19.040
All right, now let's work on this.

00:19.040 --> 00:21.780
So they already give us a
hint of how to solve it.

00:21.780 --> 00:24.540
They have the graph of y
is equal to 3/2 to the x.

00:24.540 --> 00:25.950
They graph it right over here.

00:25.950 --> 00:28.140
And this gives us a hint,
and especially because it's,

00:28.140 --> 00:30.670
they want us to find an
approximate solution,

00:30.670 --> 00:32.280
that maybe we can solve this equation

00:32.280 --> 00:35.430
or approximate a solution to
this equation through graphing.

00:35.430 --> 00:37.350
And the way we could do
that is we could take

00:37.350 --> 00:41.340
each side of this equation
and set them up as a function.

00:41.340 --> 00:44.080
We could set y equals to each side of it.

00:44.080 --> 00:46.730
So if we set y equals
to the left-hand side,

00:46.730 --> 00:51.730
we get y is equal to 3/2 to the x power,

00:51.930 --> 00:53.150
which is what they originally give us,

00:53.150 --> 00:54.350
the graph of that.

00:54.350 --> 00:57.810
And if we set y equal
to the right-hand side,

00:57.810 --> 01:00.290
we get y is equal to five.

01:00.290 --> 01:02.750
And we can graph that.

01:02.750 --> 01:05.630
And what's interesting
here is if we can find

01:05.630 --> 01:09.110
the x-value that gives us the same y-value

01:09.110 --> 01:11.370
on both of these equations,

01:11.370 --> 01:13.810
well that means that those
graphs are going to intersect.

01:13.810 --> 01:16.840
And if I'm getting the same
y-value for that x-value

01:16.840 --> 01:19.080
in both of these, well then that means

01:19.080 --> 01:22.040
that 3/2 to the x

01:22.040 --> 01:25.020
is going to be equal to five.

01:25.020 --> 01:27.500
And so we could look
at where they intersect

01:27.500 --> 01:31.560
and get an approximate sense
of what x-value that is.

01:31.560 --> 01:33.660
And we can see it, at least over here,

01:33.660 --> 01:38.020
it looks like x is roughly equal to four.

01:38.020 --> 01:42.800
So x is approximately equal to four.

01:42.800 --> 01:45.750
And if we wanted to, and
we'd be done at that point.

01:45.750 --> 01:48.380
If you wanted to, you
could try to test it out.

01:48.380 --> 01:50.307
You could say, "Hey does
that actually work out?

01:50.307 --> 01:54.360
"3/2 to the fourth power,
is that equal to five?"

01:54.360 --> 01:56.970
Let's see, three to the fourth is 81.

01:56.970 --> 01:59.210
Two to the fourth is 16.

01:59.210 --> 02:03.310
It gets us, it gets us
pretty close to five.

02:03.310 --> 02:05.970
16 times five is 80.

02:05.970 --> 02:08.790
So it's not exact, but
it gets us pretty close.

02:08.790 --> 02:10.260
And if you had a graphing calculator

02:10.260 --> 02:12.930
that could really zoom in
and zoom in and zoom in,

02:12.930 --> 02:16.060
you would get a value,
you would see that x is

02:16.060 --> 02:19.990
slightly different than x equals four.

02:19.990 --> 02:21.010
But let's do another example.

02:21.010 --> 02:23.330
The key here is that we
can approximate solutions

02:23.330 --> 02:25.203
to equations through graphing.

02:26.390 --> 02:29.650
So here we are told, this is the graph

02:29.650 --> 02:33.320
of y is equal to, so we have
this third-degree polynomial

02:33.320 --> 02:34.630
right over here.

02:34.630 --> 02:37.810
Use the graph to answer
the following questions.

02:37.810 --> 02:40.338
How many solutions does the equation

02:40.338 --> 02:43.410
x to the third minus two
x squared minus x plus one

02:43.410 --> 02:45.770
equals negative one have?

02:45.770 --> 02:47.676
Pause this video and
try to think about that.

02:47.676 --> 02:50.110
Well when we think
about solutions to this,

02:50.110 --> 02:52.960
we could say, all right, well
let's imagine two functions,

02:52.960 --> 02:56.500
one is y is equal to x to
the third minus two x squared

02:56.500 --> 03:00.120
minus x plus one, which we
already have graphed here.

03:00.120 --> 03:02.800
And let's say that the other equation

03:02.800 --> 03:05.660
or the other function is y
is equal to negative one.

03:05.660 --> 03:08.210
And then how many times
do these intersect?

03:08.210 --> 03:09.920
That would tell us how
many solutions we have.

03:09.920 --> 03:13.200
So that is y is equal to negative one.

03:13.200 --> 03:15.580
And so every time they intersect,

03:15.580 --> 03:18.030
that means we have a solution
to our original equation.

03:18.030 --> 03:21.770
And they intersect one,
two, and three times.

03:21.770 --> 03:24.340
So this has three solutions.

03:24.340 --> 03:26.300
What about the second situation?

03:26.300 --> 03:29.040
How many solutions does the
equation all of this business

03:29.040 --> 03:30.494
equal two have?

03:30.494 --> 03:33.953
Well same drill, we could set y equals to

03:33.953 --> 03:36.380
x to the third minus two x
squared minus x plus one.

03:36.380 --> 03:39.410
And then we could think
about another function,

03:39.410 --> 03:41.280
what if y is equal to two?

03:41.280 --> 03:45.640
Well y equals two would be up over there,

03:45.640 --> 03:46.860
y equals two.

03:46.860 --> 03:49.830
And we could see it
only intersects y equals

03:49.830 --> 03:51.720
all of this business once.

03:51.720 --> 03:55.380
So this is only going
to have one solution.

03:55.380 --> 03:57.440
So the key here, and
I'll just write it out,

03:57.440 --> 03:58.980
and these are screenshots
from the exercise

03:58.980 --> 04:00.750
on Khan Academy where
you'd have to type in one,

04:00.750 --> 04:03.440
or in the previous example,
you would type in four.

04:03.440 --> 04:06.130
But these are examples where
you can take an equation

04:06.130 --> 04:08.830
of one variable, set both sides of them

04:08.830 --> 04:12.090
independently equal to y, graph them,

04:12.090 --> 04:13.550
and then think about where they intersect.

04:13.550 --> 04:15.550
Because the x-values where they intersect

04:15.550 --> 04:18.440
will be solutions to
your original equation.

04:18.440 --> 04:21.690
And a graph is a useful
way of approximating

04:21.690 --> 04:23.290
what a solution will be,
especially if you have

04:23.290 --> 04:26.083
a graphing calculator or
Desmos or something like that.
